{"name":"Buckskin","hex":"#d4ba8c","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"buckskin","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The skin of a male deer, a buck.","Clothing made from buckskin."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a grayish yellow in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/buckskin"}],"entries":[{"title":"Kaurna","blocks":[{"text":"The Kaurna people are a group of Aboriginal people whose traditional lands include the Adelaide Plains of South Australia. They were known as the Adelaide tribe by the early settlers. Kaurna culture and language were almost completely destroyed within a few decades of the British colonisation of South Australia in 1836. However, extensive documentation by early missionaries and other researchers has enabled a modern revival of both language and culture. The phrase Kaurna meyunna means \"Kaurna people\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Ivaritji.jpg/330px-Ivaritji.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kaurna"},{"title":"Buckskin (film)","blocks":[{"text":"Buckskin is a 1968 American Western film directed by Michael D. Moore and starring an all-star cast. The main stars were Barry Sullivan and Joan Caulfield. Lon Chaney Jr. plays the role of Sheriff Tangley and Richard Arlen plays a townsman. The other stars were Barbara Hale, John Russell, Wendell Corey, Bill Williams, Leo Gordon, George Chandler, Aki Aleong and Barton MacLane. It was the last of the series of A.C. Lyles Westerns for Paramount. The screenwriter Michael Fisher was the son of the series screenwriter Stephen Gould Fisher. It was released by Paramount Pictures in May 1968."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/7/74/Buckskinposter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buckskin_(film)"},{"title":"Buckskin (leather)","blocks":[{"text":"Buckskin is the soft, pliable, porous preserved hide of an animal—usually deer—tanned in the same way as deerskin clothing worn by Native Americans. Some leather sold as \"buckskin\" may now be sheepskin tanned with modern chromate tanning chemicals and dyed to resemble real buckskin."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Deerskin%2C_Kelvingrove_Museum%2C_Glasgow_-_DSC06237.JPG/330px-Deerskin%2C_Kelvingrove_Museum%2C_Glasgow_-_DSC06237.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buckskin_(leather)"},{"title":"Buckskin (horse)","blocks":[{"text":"Buckskin is a colour of horse. Buckskin coloring is a hair coat color referring to a color that resembles certain shades of tanned deerskin. Similar colors in some breeds of dogs are also called buckskin. The horse has a tan or gold coat with black points. Buckskin occurs as a result of the cream dilution gene acting on a bay horse. Therefore, a buckskin has the Extension, or \"black base coat\" (E) gene, the agouti gene (A) gene, which restricts the black base coat to the points, and one copy of the cream gene (CCr), which lightens the red/brown color of the bay coat to a tan/gold."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/31/Buckskin_New_Forest_pony.JPG/330px-Buckskin_New_Forest_pony.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Buckskin_(horse)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Kaurnaland.png/330px-Kaurnaland.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Kaurnaland.png/330px-Kaurnaland.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Approximate extent of Kaurna territory, based on the description by Amery (2000)","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKaurnaland.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Onkaparinga_Estuary_2.jpg/500px-Onkaparinga_Estuary_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Onkaparinga_Estuary_2.jpg/1280px-Onkaparinga_Estuary_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f6/Onkaparinga_Estuary_2.jpg/500px-Onkaparinga_Estuary_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The name of the Onkaparinga River (pictured) is derived from the Kaurna language.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOnkaparinga_Estuary_2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Murlapaka_-_Kaurna_shield.jpg/500px-Murlapaka_-_Kaurna_shield.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Murlapaka_-_Kaurna_shield.jpg/1280px-Murlapaka_-_Kaurna_shield.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5f/Murlapaka_-_Kaurna_shield.jpg/500px-Murlapaka_-_Kaurna_shield.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Murlapaka shield, a shield created in the 1800s by unknown Kaurna person","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMurlapaka_-_Kaurna_shield.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Shirt_for_Chief%27s_War_Dress%2C_19th_century%2C_50.67.1a.jpg/500px-Shirt_for_Chief%27s_War_Dress%2C_19th_century%2C_50.67.1a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Shirt_for_Chief%27s_War_Dress%2C_19th_century%2C_50.67.1a.jpg/1280px-Shirt_for_Chief%27s_War_Dress%2C_19th_century%2C_50.67.1a.jpg?ut